\section{Specify Which Round Trip Is Required}

A printer/parser pair can be tested for preserving an IR up to renaming of temporary values and normalization of the text. A lowering/raising pair has a different obligation: not necessarily reproducing the same program structure, but reproducing the observable meaning in a specified environment. For example, Necula and colleagues' C Intermediate Language normalizes constructs and can emit readable C again. The emitted C need not have the original grouping of expressions or original local names. If the output is intended for further editing, fidelity to source structure becomes yet another requirement; semantic equivalence alone does not recover the author's intent.

The dossier's Markdown Chapters~05, \textit{Reverse Compilation}, and 06, \textit{Bidirectional Compilers}, make different uses of a reverse direction. At a serialization boundary write the law against an explicit normalization \(N\): parsing the printed IR yields an IR equivalent to \(N\) of the input under the chosen structural equality. At a semantic boundary, specify an observation set: returns, visible updates, exceptions, external events, and any termination behavior the language guarantees. Do not require equality on executions outside the source's defined domain. When the semantics is nondeterministic and the language permits a compiler to reduce the choices, target behaviors must refine, rather than necessarily equal, source behaviors on the relevant domain.

\section{Worked Validation: Loop or Closed Form?}

Consider validating a round trip that reconstructs \texttt{return 2*n} in place of a loop that starts with \((i,s)=(0,0)\) and repeatedly increments \(i\) by one and \(s\) by two until \(i=n\). Suppose \(n\) is a nonnegative signed 32-bit input, both source additions and the reconstructed multiplication are checked, and there are no external effects except a generic overflow trap. For \(n\le 2^{30}-1\) the coupling invariant is
\[
  0\le i\le n,\qquad s=2i,\qquad
  s+2(n-i)=2n ,
\]
with the assertions interpreted over mathematical integers. The first two conjuncts are established by initialization and preserved by each iteration; the third compares the completed sum predicted by the current state with the target result. The variant \(n-i\) decreases at each iteration. At termination \(i=n\), giving the same returned value. For \(n\ge 2^{30}\), the source reaches the \(2^{30}\)-th increment of the accumulator and overflows there. The target overflows in its multiplication. They are equivalent in this case \emph{only} because the trap class is the same and the iteration count and location of the trap are not observations. If the reconstructed operation were wrapping, the input \(n=2^{30}\) would instead return \(-2^{31}\).

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-200} separates the coupling argument on representable inputs from the trap agreement on the remaining inputs.

