\section{Check the Printer Against a Canonical Form}

Choose a separate structural oracle for a small serialization format:
one pure straight-line block with typed parameters, instructions in
definition order, and a return. Renaming temporary values is immaterial;
parameter order, operation order, literal values, and arithmetic modes
are not. The normalizer below is intentionally weaker than an optimizer:
it does not commute operands or remove instructions. Run it on the
original and on the parsed result of printing that original. In both
cases validate the structure first, so canonicalization need not give
meaning to forward references or duplicate definitions.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Well-formed block $B$
\Let{N}{\operatorname{parameterNumbers}(B)}
\Let{R}{[\operatorname{parameterTypes}(B)]}
\ForAll{instructions $i$ in definition order}
  \To{Resolve operands before assigning the result number.}
  \Let{a}{\operatorname{encodeOperands}(i,N)}
  \Let{k}{|N|}
  \To{Operator attributes are part of the record.}
  \State Append $(k,\operatorname{op}(i),\operatorname{attrs}(i),a)$ to $R$
  \Let{N[i.\mathrm{result}]}{k}
\EndFor
\State Append $\operatorname{encodeReturn}(B,N)$ to $R$
\State \Return $R$
\end{sudocode}

For one eight-bit parameter and the instruction
\texttt{t = add.wrap(p, 3)}, the parameter is numbered $0$, the result
is numbered $1$, and the return refers to $1$. A parsed block that calls
the result \texttt{v7} produces the same record sequence; a printer
that drops the wrapping attribute and reparses as checked addition
does not. At input $254$, unsigned wrapping addition returns $1$ while
checked addition traps. That execution is useful triage, but even
without running it, the structural test should identify exactly which
instruction record changed. Encode constants distinctly from references;
literal $0$ is not reference $0$.

After each instruction, every canonical number names one earlier
definition and every encoded reference selects the same binding as
before canonicalization. With a name table the pass visits each
operand once. This method does not extend unchanged to arbitrary CFGs:
block ordering and edge identities must also be normalized without
losing the predecessor map for merges. It is also intentionally strict
about the chosen structural law. An object that inserts a redundant
instruction may preserve behavior and still fail this serializer test,
whereas a lowering and raising audit might accept it.

\section{Locate the Boundary of the Claim}

Well-formedness checking should still precede semantic comparison: verify symbol resolution, operand types, and the representation's control and binding invariants. It provides sharper diagnostics than a solver query on malformed IR, but neither of the two example fragments fails such a check merely because it uses the wrong arithmetic variant. Garzella, Baranowski, He, and Rakamari\'c show in \textit{Leveraging Compiler Intermediate Representation for Multi- and Cross-Language Verification} that merely targeting LLVM IR does not make existing verification support transfer automatically: missing instruction semantics and missing runtime or library models matter.

Record which observations and environment were modeled, which preconditions were proved rather than assumed, and which obligations timed out or were left to testing. The dossier's Markdown Chapter~39, \textit{Semantic Fingerprints and Metamorphic Compiler Testing}, supplies a complementary testing strategy: for example, renaming temporaries should leave behavior unchanged and should not defeat a structural round-trip test based on canonical names. Distinguishing tested equivalence from proved refinement makes even a failed round trip useful: it can be diagnosed as lost structure, a bad model, or a genuine change of behavior.
